  "slug": "3d-pose-browser-d480",
  "summary": "Five preprints apply automated tracking, information theory, LLM interfaces and vocalization analysis to social and communicative phenotypes in mice.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is automated, quantitative phenotyping of social and communicative behavior in mice. LMT-MCP links the Live Mouse Tracker database to LLMs for natural-language behavioral analysis, while an RFID home-cage approach uses mutual information on positional data to quantify social dependence, validated against three-chamber sociability. Applied to genetic models, Shank3 knockouts show heightened engagement with familiar cagemates but altered, more antagonistic behavior toward strangers. Two preprints address neonatal ultrasonic vocalizations: one analyzes about 59,000 pup calls across postnatal days 0-5, finding age- and context-dependent call-type shifts, and Foxg1 models show opposing call-duration effects that depend on the mutation. The items emphasize scalable readouts and developmental or context dependence rather than a shared 3D pose method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
